WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.320
In 1951, doctors took a tissue sample from
a poor, dying black woman that was given to

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.350
researchers without her knowledge or permission.

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:12.370
The HeLa cells, named for the patient’s
first and last name, acted differently than

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:16.910
other cells in the lab — they were hardier,
and replicated at an amazing rate.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:18.630
They also didn’t die.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:23.830
Cultivation of HeLa launched a revolution
in biomedical research and a multibillion-dollar

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:24.830
industry.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:29.500
The cells were sold all over the world, and
led to important advances to fight humankind’s

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.750
deadliest diseases, including polio, HIV,
and cancer.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:38.410
No one, aside from the patient’s doctors
and lead researchers knew the origins of the

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:42.120
HeLa cells — not her name, or anything else
about her life.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.010
Two decades passed before this mystery woman
was revealed.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:49.910
And more years still, before her family had
answers to their burning questions.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:57.580
Today, we examine the woman behind the notorious
and immortal HeLa cells: Henrietta Lacks.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:14.700
Early Life
On August 1, 1920, Eliza Lacks Pleasant gave

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.290
birth to a baby girl, Loretta, in a shack
at the end of dead-end road in Roanoke, Virginia.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:24.510
At some point during her life Loretta changed
her name to Henrietta, though no one knows

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:25.510
when or why.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:29.920
The shack, overlooking the train depot, was
the young girl’s home until her mother tragically

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:30.920
died.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:33.360
Henrietta was four years old.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.290
Eliza passed from this life to the next during
childbirth with her tenth baby, leaving her

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.210
husband Johnny a widow.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:45.000
Unable to care for all the children, he sent
them away to live with family, 125 miles southeast

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.380
from Roanoke in southern Virginia.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:52.280
The Lacks family lived in the town of Clover,
just past Difficult Creek on the banks of

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.550
the River of Death.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:58.509
It sounds like a bad omen, but in the 1920s
it looked a lot like most small towns in rural

00:01:58.509 --> 00:01:59.509
Virginia.

00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:03.790
On the outskirts, fields of tobacco and old
plantation houses dotted the rolling hills.

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:07.150
And on Main Street down the dusty road, the
town buzzed with life.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:11.060
There was a movie theater, grocery store,
post office, shops, restaurants, and a train

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:12.060
depot.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.190
Henrietta wouldn’t recognize the town today;
Clover lost so many residents by the late

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:18.849
1990s it’s official town charter was revoked.

00:02:18.849 --> 00:02:25.010
In the last census in 2010, the total population
had dwindled to a mere 438.

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:29.900
When Johnny came to town in 1924, Henrietta
and her nine brothers and sisters had to be

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.760
divided up amongst the relatives.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.140
No one could take all ten children.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:39.020
Henrietta ended up living with her grandfather,
Tommy Lacks, in a four-room log cabin called

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:40.220
the home-house.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:44.890
Generations of Lackses had lived and died
there, buried in the small family plot Henrietta

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:46.510
could see out her window.

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:50.330
Formerly slave quarters, the home-house was
a simple structure with no electricity or

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:55.730
indoor plumbing, only gas lanterns and water
that Henrietta hauled up from the creek below.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:00.099
Tommy was already caring for another grandchild,
nine-year old “Day” Lacks when Henrietta

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:01.760
came to live at the home-house.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.160
The children shared a room, and worked every
day together planting and harvesting the tobacco,

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.349
and tending to the animals and garden.

00:03:08.349 --> 00:03:12.150
After the day’s work, they sometimes swam
in the swimming hole, had bonfires at night,

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.710
and played ring around the rosy with their
cousins.

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:18.700
Once a month during harvest grampa Tommy and
the cousins headed to South Boston, to the

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:22.140
second largest tobacco market in the nation,
to sell their crop.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.260
It was a time they all looked forward too
even if the market wasn’t a safe place for

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:29.700
children with its free-flowing booze, prostitution,
and the occasional murder.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:34.030
At the home-house, Henrietta awoke every morning
at four o’clock to milk the cows and feed

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:35.030
the pigs.

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:38.260
When she was finished her morning chores,
she walked two miles to the schoolhouse for

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:42.440
colored children, past the white school where
children threw rocks at her and called her

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:43.440
names.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.290
Jim Crow laws segregated blacks from whites,
and they remained in place until 1964.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:51.880
In the short 31 years of her life, it was
all she knew.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:55.440
By sixth grade, Henrietta walked to the schoolhouse
one last time.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.470
Like Day and the other Lackses, she dropped
out to work in the tobacco fields.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:09.799
“Lovey Dovey” Woman

00:04:09.799 --> 00:04:14.060
Few public photos of Henrietta Lacks exist
-- the most well-known is a striking image

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:19.389
from a 1970s Rolling Stone article of a smiling,
smartly-dressed woman in a skirt, suit jacket,

00:04:19.389 --> 00:04:20.889
and open toe sandals.

00:04:20.889 --> 00:04:24.740
Her hands are firmly placed on her hips, and
her walnut eyes glimmer at the camera.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:28.610
She looks self-assured with a hint of mischief
spread out in her grin.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:32.529
It’s obvious, even by today’s standards,
Henrietta was beautiful.

00:04:32.529 --> 00:04:36.300
She was the envy of other women, and desirable
to all the men around her.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.220
It wasn’t only her beauty that attracted
people to Henrietta.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:43.719
Her cousin “Cootie” described her as “lovey
dovey” — as kind as she was pretty — always

00:04:43.719 --> 00:04:46.180
taking care of family and friends in need.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:49.680
As a child, Cootie had been stricken with
polio and Henrietta took care of him when

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:50.889
it got bad.

00:04:50.889 --> 00:04:56.059
Everyone who knew her, remembers Henrietta
as a gentle caregiver and a strong woman.

00:04:56.059 --> 00:05:00.309
Even when cancer was spreading inside her
body, she didn’t make a fuss or draw attention

00:05:00.309 --> 00:05:01.309
to herself.

00:05:01.309 --> 00:05:04.699
Very few people knew, until the end, how she
suffered.

00:05:04.699 --> 00:05:08.129
Henrietta had no shortage of men vying for
her affection as a young woman.

00:05:08.129 --> 00:05:12.099
Henrietta chose Day, her cousin with whom
she shared a bedroom and had spent countless

00:05:12.099 --> 00:05:13.889
hours with since the age of four.

00:05:13.889 --> 00:05:18.469
They had their first baby, a boy named Lawrence,
out of wedlock when Henrietta was barely fourteen

00:05:18.469 --> 00:05:19.469
years old.

00:05:19.469 --> 00:05:23.539
He was born on the home-house floor, like
his father and numerous cousins, aunts and

00:05:23.539 --> 00:05:25.270
uncles, and grandparents before.

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:27.650
Another Lacks baby, Elsie, was born four years
later.

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:32.099
Eventually Henrietta and Day said their vows
in a private marriage ceremony on April 10,

00:05:32.099 --> 00:05:33.099
1941.

00:05:33.099 --> 00:05:38.349
She was 21 and he was 25.

00:05:38.349 --> 00:05:45.810
Turner Station

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:50.729
By the winter of 1941, the country was at
war and small tobacco farms including Henrietta

00:05:50.729 --> 00:05:53.469
and Day’s, were barely making enough to
make ends meet.

00:05:53.469 --> 00:05:58.469
But to the north, there was opportunity in
the booming factories, as demand for steel

00:05:58.469 --> 00:05:59.469
increased.

00:05:59.469 --> 00:06:04.099
A great migration of black families came to
Turner Station, Maryland, leaving behind the

00:06:04.099 --> 00:06:05.340
southern farms.

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:09.699
At Bethlehem Steel’s Sparrow Point in Baltimore
County, a black man could make up to eighty

00:06:09.699 --> 00:06:12.520
cents an hour — the most any of the Lackses
had seen.

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:16.780
In the factories, black men took the jobs
white men rejected and they were exposed to

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:20.300
a host of toxic chemicals, including asbestos.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:24.069
When the men finished their shifts and returned
home, the toxins were shared with their wives

00:06:24.069 --> 00:06:26.490
and children through their clothes.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:30.401
At the urging of a Lacks’ cousin who was
doing well for himself at Sparrow Point, Day

00:06:30.401 --> 00:06:34.620
and Henrietta moved with their babies to Turner
Station, starting a new life.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:38.439
Other cousins made the transition too, including
Henrietta’s close friend Sadie.

00:06:38.439 --> 00:06:42.810
The city was a change for the Lackses but
they adjusted and had fun.

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:46.529
After cleaning, cooking, and taking care of
the children, Henrietta and Sadie would put

00:06:46.529 --> 00:06:49.339
their dancing shoes on and sneak out until
the wee hours.

00:06:49.339 --> 00:06:51.930
The two women boogied, laughed, and had a
great time.

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:55.990
And, when they weren’t dancing, Henrietta,
Sadie, and Sadie’s sister Margaret played

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:58.090
Bingo in Henrietta’s living room.

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:02.169
Turner Station was new and exciting but Henrietta
preferred the country.

00:07:02.169 --> 00:07:05.639
Whenever she could, she rode the train back
to Clover to work in the fields, or churn

00:07:05.639 --> 00:07:07.479
butter on the home-house porch.

00:07:07.479 --> 00:07:10.659
It was here her eldest daughter, Elsie, was
at ease.

00:07:10.659 --> 00:07:14.869
Elsie had inherited her mother’s good looks
but was born “special,” unable to speak

00:07:14.869 --> 00:07:15.869
or talk.

00:07:15.869 --> 00:07:20.389
She made chirps and caw sounds like the birds
and sometimes flailed her arms in front of

00:07:20.389 --> 00:07:21.389
her face.

00:07:21.389 --> 00:07:26.009
Years later it was understood Elsie has epilepsy,
and cognitive, developmental delays that left

00:07:26.009 --> 00:07:27.599
her unable to communicate.

00:07:27.599 --> 00:07:30.139
No one knew exactly what went on in her pretty
head.

00:07:30.139 --> 00:07:34.679
At home in Turner Station, Elsie was known
to occasionally run out into the street screaming.

00:07:34.679 --> 00:07:38.810
By the time Henrietta was pregnant with her
fifth baby, Elsie had grown stronger, and

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:41.690
this made it near impossible for her mother
to care for her.

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:47.029
To make life easier, doctors recommended Henrietta
commit Elsie to the Crownsville State Hospital,

00:07:47.029 --> 00:07:49.469
formerly the Hospital for the Negro Insane.

00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:53.209
Believing the doctors’ knew best, Henrietta
institutionalized her daughter.

00:07:53.209 --> 00:07:57.089
Years later, family members said “a part
of Henrietta died the day she left Elsie at

00:07:57.089 --> 00:07:58.089
Crownsville.”

00:07:58.089 --> 00:08:02.469
Henrietta routinely visited her daughter once
a week at the hospital before becoming ill.

00:08:02.469 --> 00:08:08.379
And after Henrietta died, no one went to Crownsville
to see Elsie again.

00:08:08.379 --> 00:08:16.449
“A Knot Inside”

00:08:16.449 --> 00:08:20.871
Henrietta was certain there was something
wrong a year and a half before her official

00:08:20.871 --> 00:08:22.889
cervical cancer diagnosis.

00:08:22.889 --> 00:08:27.229
She shared her worry with her cousins Sadie
and Margaret, saying there was a “knot inside”

00:08:27.229 --> 00:08:31.659
and explaining how, “It hurt somethin awful
— when that man want to get with me.

00:08:31.659 --> 00:08:34.039
Sweet Jesus aren’t them but some pains.”

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:37.340
The women poked at her stomach over and over
and felt nothing.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:39.990
Maybe it was a baby growing inside, they suggested.

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:43.871
To be sure nothing was seriously wrong, her
cousins tried to convince Henrietta to see

00:08:43.871 --> 00:08:45.680
a doctor but she didn’t.

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:48.339
And about a week later, Henrietta found out
she was pregnant.

00:08:48.339 --> 00:08:53.139
The ladies reassured her it was just the baby
making her feel funny but Henrietta wasn’t

00:08:53.139 --> 00:08:54.139
convinced.

00:08:54.139 --> 00:08:57.610
Nevertheless, she didn’t bring it up again
with Sadie and Margaret and the women never

00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:00.100
mentioned the conversation with anyone else.

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:03.100
Henrietta carried her fifth baby to full-term,
and a soon after Joe was born, Henrietta was

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:04.270
not feeling any better.

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:08.420
One day, alone in the bathroom, she slipped
into the bathtub and reached inside herself.

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:12.120
She could feel “the knot,” and told Day
she needed to go see the doctor.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:16.810
On a referral from a local physician, Henrietta
went to Johns Hopkins Hospital to seek answers.

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:20.440
At this point, Henrietta thought the doctors
would “fix her right up.”

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:25.550
On January 29, 1951, Day drove Henrietta with
their three small children, 20 miles from

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:27.510
Turner Station to Johns Hopkins.

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:31.700
Johns Hopkins was not only a hospital, it
was a renowned medical research center.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:35.690
It also had a public ward, serving patients
who were too poor to afford treatment — many

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:36.889
of whom were black.

00:09:36.889 --> 00:09:41.570
In the 1950s, under discriminatory segregation
laws in the U.S., black patients could be

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:46.480
turned away at hospitals, doctor’s offices,
and clinics that served white patients.

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:49.120
Sometimes the consequences were dire, including
death.

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:54.060
Johns Hopkins was Henrietta’s best, and
only, real option for diagnosis and treatment

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:55.790
of cervical cancer.

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:59.540
While Day sat in the car with their small
children, waiting for Henrietta to return,

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:03.130
she met Dr. Howard Jones, the gynecologist
on duty.

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:06.850
In the exam room, he reviewed her medical
history and listened to Henrietta describe

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:08.680
the lump she felt inside her.

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:11.990
When Jones examined her, he found a hard mass
about the size of a nickel.

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:16.399
The tumor was the color of “grape Jell-O,”
exactly in the spot Henrietta said it would

00:10:16.399 --> 00:10:17.410
be.

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:22.100
Decades later in his nineties, Jones recalled
never seeing anything like Henrietta’s tumor

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:26.410
during his years practicing medicine — not
before or since.

00:10:26.410 --> 00:10:30.769
During the exam, Dr. Jones cut a tiny sample
of the tumor to test, and confirm what he

00:10:30.769 --> 00:10:32.670
suspected was cancerous.

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:36.100
Then he sent Henrietta back home to Turner
Station to await the results.

00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:40.750
Jones thought it was odd Henrietta had a full-term
baby just a few months prior, with no notes

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:45.500
mentioning the tumor, or any abnormalities
of the cervix listed in her chart.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:49.589
It was unlikely physicians would have missed
seeing it, which meant it must have grown

00:10:49.589 --> 00:10:51.100
at an alarming rate.

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:55.870
A few days later, Henrietta received the results
back from the lab, “Epidermoid carcinoma

00:10:55.870 --> 00:11:01.020
of the cervix, Stage I.”

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:05.990
Growing “Like Crabgrass”

00:11:05.990 --> 00:11:12.980
In 1951, it was standard practice to conduct
research on public ward hospital patients

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:14.329
without consent.

00:11:14.329 --> 00:11:18.500
Explicit permission, or “informed consent,”
from patients wasn’t a term that was coined,

00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:22.390
and no ethical or legal standard existed in
the U.S. for obtaining it.

00:11:22.390 --> 00:11:27.680
At Hopkins, the then-leading cervical cancer
expert in the world, and Dr. Jones’ boss,

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:32.060
Richard TeLinde, believed that since the patients
were treated free of charge, the research

00:11:32.060 --> 00:11:34.970
was a form of payment for the hospital’s
services.

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:39.000
When Henrietta went in for her exam, a sample
from Henrietta’s tumor was sent to George

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.899
Gey’s lab, the head of tissue culture research
at Hopkins, without her knowledge.

00:11:42.899 --> 00:11:47.899
For years Gey had been trying unsuccessfully
to grow, and keep alive, human cells outside

00:11:47.899 --> 00:11:49.899
the body in petri dishes.

00:11:49.899 --> 00:11:54.300
Culturing cells allowed scientists to test
their response to all kinds of drugs, diseases,

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:57.660
and conditions in order to understand how
the human body would react.

00:11:57.660 --> 00:12:00.670
And doing so didn’t cause harm to patients.

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:04.191
When Henrietta went to Hopkins, an agreement
was already in place to provide Gey’s research

00:12:04.191 --> 00:12:09.600
lab with patients’ cancerous tumor samples,
primarily to be used in studies to prove TeLinde’s

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:10.600
theories.

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:15.170
TeLinde was a believer that of the two types
of cervical cancer, the non-invasive variety

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:18.450
(carcinoma in situ), could spread and become
deadly.

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:22.420
This was contrary to what the majority of
researchers thought at the time, and physicians’

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:26.910
practice followed this conclusion — treating
only the invasive cancers aggressively.

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:31.880
He had another motive for his research using
cells too — TeLinde wanted to reduce the

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:37.110
number of “unjustifiable hysterectomies”
by detecting what wasn’t cervical cancer

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:38.110
at all.

00:12:38.110 --> 00:12:42.120
The PAP smear test, used to identify changes
to a woman’s cervical cells, was developed

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:43.279
only a decade earlier.

00:12:43.279 --> 00:12:47.220
But, few physicians could interpret the results
and treat patients accordingly.

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:52.350
In extreme cases, a woman needing only antibiotics
to clear up a minor infection, might have

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:55.860
her entire uterus removed in a surgical procedure.

00:12:55.860 --> 00:13:00.440
When Gey’s lab assistant Mary received Henrietta’s
tissue sample, she had every reason to believe

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:04.120
the cells, if they lived at all, would die
within a few days.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:06.889
She was eating lunch, and decided to finish
her sandwich first.

00:13:06.889 --> 00:13:11.600
So far, even with Gey’s standards, meticulous
process, and emphasis on sterilization, his

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:14.630
lab had little success with growing human
cells.

00:13:14.630 --> 00:13:18.509
When Mary got to work on Henrietta’s cancer
cells, she carefully and precisely sliced

00:13:18.509 --> 00:13:23.060
the samples with steady hands, mixed the media
the cells would need to grow, and labeled

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:27.089
them “HeLa” — for the first two letters
of Henrietta’s first and last names.

00:13:27.089 --> 00:13:30.470
Then, she left them alone, not expecting much.

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:34.420
By the next morning to Mary’s surprise,
Henrietta’s cells had divided, and in the

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:37.870
days ahead, HeLa cells kept making copies
of themselves.

00:13:37.870 --> 00:13:43.069
They grew unlike any cells before, and expanded
into as much space as Mary would give them.

00:13:43.069 --> 00:13:48.200
Gey was ecstatic the cells were growing “like
crabgrass” and soon, among researchers,

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:50.779
word was out about the amazing HeLa cells.

00:13:50.779 --> 00:13:55.579
Everyone knew the implications for furthering
biomedical research, and this was Gey’s

00:13:55.579 --> 00:13:56.579
goal.

00:13:56.579 --> 00:14:07.889
Soon, he began sending HeLa cells to any scientist
interested in using them for their own studies.

00:14:07.889 --> 00:14:13.999
Death
Henrietta’s treatment for cervical cancer

00:14:13.999 --> 00:14:17.980
was radium, a radioactive metal that glows
blue and is highly toxic.

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:22.569
In the early twentieth century, upon radium’s
discovery, it was hailed as a miracle to cure

00:14:22.569 --> 00:14:24.670
all kinds of ailments.

00:14:24.670 --> 00:14:29.790
Radium kills cancer cells...but it can burn
skin from a person’s body in high enough

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:30.790
quantities.

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:34.329
Being radioactive, radium also causes cancer.

00:14:34.329 --> 00:14:39.329
In 1951, the radium treatment for cervical
cancer involved placing tubes inside the patient

00:14:39.329 --> 00:14:41.139
next to the affected area.

00:14:41.139 --> 00:14:44.829
Common side effects include nausea, vomiting,
weakness, and anemia.

00:14:44.829 --> 00:14:49.459
If Henrietta experienced any of these, she
didn’t let on or seem sick.

00:14:49.459 --> 00:14:53.470
After treatments started, Henrietta finally
told the same cousins she confided in about

00:14:53.470 --> 00:14:54.660
“the knot” inside her.

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:58.949
She didn’t want anyone to worry, but the
frequent visits to Johns Hopkins would mean

00:14:58.949 --> 00:15:01.779
she would be away and people would wonder
why.

00:15:01.779 --> 00:15:06.350
Initially the radium treatments seemed to
work and doctors recorded the tumor had vanished.

00:15:06.350 --> 00:15:10.930
As therapy progressed Henrietta didn’t feel
better, and to her horror, her abdomen turned

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:13.529
from soft brown to black as coal.

00:15:13.529 --> 00:15:17.730
One day she lifted up her shirt and showed
Sadie and Margaret, and said it felt as though

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:20.459
“the blackness be spreading all inside.”

00:15:20.459 --> 00:15:22.910
The radium treatments didn’t cure Henrietta’s
cancer.

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:27.880
By the fall of 1951, her body was full of
tumors — in her diaphragm, bladder, cervix,

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:32.139
kidneys, and lungs and she was in excruciating
pain.

00:15:32.139 --> 00:15:37.379
She lay in bed at Johns Hopkins and scream
out like it was “the devil of pain itself.”

00:15:37.379 --> 00:15:41.809
Between the treatments and the cancer, Henrietta
was weak and her kidneys were no longer flushing

00:15:41.809 --> 00:15:43.839
out dangerous toxins in her blood.

00:15:43.839 --> 00:15:45.850
She had to receive numerous transfusions.

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:49.779
The doctors tried different medications to
ease her suffering, including morphine, which

00:15:49.779 --> 00:15:51.439
didn’t touch the pain.

00:15:51.439 --> 00:15:55.990
On one desperate attempt, the doctors even
injected alcohol into her spine but nothing

00:15:55.990 --> 00:15:57.079
seemed to work.

00:15:57.079 --> 00:16:00.700
All anyone could do was sit beside her bed
and pray.

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:04.540
Henrietta’s sister Gladys was at her bedside
the night she succumbed to cancer.

00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:08.459
Knowing her time had come, Henrietta made
her sister promise to take care of her kids

00:16:08.459 --> 00:16:12.749
and whispered through tears, “Don’t you
let anything bad happen to them children when

00:16:12.749 --> 00:16:13.749
I’m gone.”

00:16:13.749 --> 00:16:19.660
At 12:15 a.m. on October 4, 1951, Henrietta
Lacks took her last breath and died in her

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:20.660
hospital bed.

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:28.939
Her body was sent back to Clover, and buried
in the family plot in an unmarked grave next

00:16:28.939 --> 00:16:32.689
to her mother.

00:16:32.689 --> 00:16:36.439
Importance of HeLa

00:16:36.439 --> 00:16:40.060
The importance of Henrietta’s cells cannot
be overstated.

00:16:40.060 --> 00:16:45.279
Through this immortalized, continuously cultured
cell line, cancer research has advanced, vaccines

00:16:45.279 --> 00:16:49.949
have been developed, gene mapping has taken
place, and treatments of various diseases

00:16:49.949 --> 00:16:51.589
have been studied and undertaken.

00:16:51.589 --> 00:16:55.920
HeLa cells have even been put into space on
missions with both Americans and Russians,

00:16:55.920 --> 00:17:00.750
were tested against an atomic bomb, and were
the first human cells to be cloned.

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:04.990
Perhaps the most well-known advances using
HeLa occurred soon after Henrietta’s death,

00:17:04.990 --> 00:17:06.890
in the midst of a polio epidemic.

00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:11.179
The virus was sweeping the country and parents
feared for their children’s lives.

00:17:11.179 --> 00:17:15.280
In late summer, dubbed “polio season”
public swimming pools were shut down and officials

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:19.350
warned the virus could spread by sitting too
close to others at movie theaters.

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:25.039
By 1952, 60,000 children were infected and
thousands were paralyzed.

00:17:25.039 --> 00:17:30.120
A vaccine was in development by Jonas Salk,
but he needed a way to test it in a lab on

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:32.710
a grand scale before injecting children.

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:34.810
HeLa provided the perfect opportunity.

00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:39.650
Soon, the HeLa Project at Tuskegee University
began growing the cell line, employing mostly

00:17:39.650 --> 00:17:41.220
black women lab technicians.

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:45.190
Tuskegee’s mass-production of HeLa would
serve as the first of its kind.

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:49.580
And, it helped launch a multibillion-dollar
industry when the first commercial cell culture

00:17:49.580 --> 00:17:54.919
operation began using Tuskegee’s model in
an old Fritos factory in Bethesda, Maryland.

00:17:54.919 --> 00:17:58.940
While this groundbreaking biomedical research
was taking place, only a handful of people

00:17:58.940 --> 00:18:04.289
could trace the HeLa cell line origins back
to a poor, 31-year old black woman with cervical

00:18:04.289 --> 00:18:05.289
cancer.

00:18:05.289 --> 00:18:19.649
Two decades would pass before the name, Henrietta
Lacks, was finally released to the public.

00:18:19.649 --> 00:18:21.191
The Lacks Family
For many years, members of the Lacks family

00:18:21.191 --> 00:18:26.340
never knew how Henrietta’s cells were used
to advance medical research and when her name

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:29.560
finally became public, there were many questions
left unanswered.

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:32.720
The children, especially her daughter Deborah,
was “too young to remember” her mother

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:37.090
and longed to know what she was like, what
her favorite color was, and whether or not

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:38.149
she breastfed her.

00:18:38.149 --> 00:18:41.799
Not knowing, and not understanding how her
mother’s cells could still be living after

00:18:41.799 --> 00:18:46.040
her death was terrifying and caused the family
great anxiety and pain.

00:18:46.040 --> 00:18:50.860
Her sons, and husband Day, were mostly angry
with Johns Hopkins, the doctors, and George

00:18:50.860 --> 00:18:55.780
Gey. Day never gave the hospital permission
to use his wife’s cells and it didn’t

00:18:55.780 --> 00:19:00.159
seem right, or fair, that people were making
millions of dollars from selling them while

00:19:00.159 --> 00:19:03.470
members of the family couldn’t even afford
health insurance.

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:07.130
Hopkins has made public statements that the
hospital has never directly profited from

00:19:07.130 --> 00:19:11.570
HeLa and at the time, no federal regulations
were in place for obtaining permission to

00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:13.860
use Henrietta’s cells for research.

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:18.700
A book published in 2010 by author Rebecca
Skloot and a movie starring Oprah Winfrey

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:23.730
has recently brought greater attention to
the woman behind the “immortal” HeLa cells,

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:26.120
raising ethical questions for the world to
ponder.

00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:31.320
For now, over sixty years after her death,
Henrietta’s cells live on and continue to

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:36.510
provide us with immeasurable value in the
fight to cure and eradicate disease.

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:40.860
For that, we can be grateful and finally know
a little bit more about the woman, Henrietta

00:19:40.860 --> 00:20:14.240
Lacks.

